We separate into cases based on the order of  $n$  mod  $210$ , from where we can sum to get the answer. Notice that 
\begin{align*}
\text{ord}_2 n = 1 
\text{ord}_3 n \in \{1, 2\} 
\text{ord}_5 n \in \{1, 2, 4\} 
\text{ord}_7 n \in \{1, 2, 3, 6\}.
\end{align*}
Furthermore, there are two choices for  $n$  for  $\text{ord}_5 n = 4$ ,  $\text{ord}_7 n = 3$ , and  $\text{ord}_7 n = 6$ . The cases are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item If the order is  $1$ , then there is just  $1$  case.
\item If the order is  $3$ , there are  $1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 2 = 2$  ways to choose the residues.
\item If the order is  $2$ , there are  $2^3 - 1 = 7$  ways to choose the residues.
\item If the order is  $4$ , there are  $2^3 = 8$  ways.
\item If the order is  $6$ , there are  $2 \cdot(2 \cdot 2 - 1) + 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 = 14$  ways depending on whether we choose an order of  $3$  or  $6$  modulo  $7$ .
\item If the order is  $12$ , there are  $2 \cdot 4 \cdot 2 = 16$  cases.
\end{itemize}
Thus the sum of the  $c_i$  is   $$ 1 \cdot 1 + 3 \cdot 2 + 7 \cdot 2 + 8 \cdot 4 + 14 \cdot 6 + 16 \cdot 12 = \boxed{329}. $$

