Let \(b=f(a)\), \(c=f(b)\), \(a=f(c)\), which are given to be positive in #3. Observe that we have \begin{align*}     b(a^2+a-1)&=1     \implies b+1&=ab(a+1).\end{align*}
Analogously,
\begin{align*}     c+1&=bc(b+1)     a+1&=ca(c+1). \end{align*} Multiplying these together, we obtain \(abc=1\).

It follows that \begin{align*} a^2+a=\frac1b+1&=ca+1,\end{align*}
and analogously
\begin{align*} b^2+b&=ab+1 c^2+c&=bc+1. \end{align*}

Summing, we have \[\left(a^2+b^2+c^2\right)+(a+b+c)=(ab+bc+ca)+3.\] However \(a^2+b^2+c^2\ge ab+bc+ca\) and \(a+b+c\ge3\), so equality must hold, implying \(a=b=c=1\).

  $$ \begin {aligned}
2\mathcal{I}&=\int_0^1 f(x)\mathrm {d}x+\int_0^1 f(x)\mathrm{d}x\quad\text {u=1-x}
&=\int_0^1 f(x)\mathrm{d}x-\int_0^1 f(1-u)\mathrm{d}u\quad\text {u=x}
&=\int_0^1\left[f(x)+f(1-x)\right]\mathrm{d}x\underbrace\geqslant_{u=x,i=1-x}\int_0^1\left|1-2x\right|\mathrm{d}x
&=\int_0^{\tfrac 12}(1-2x)\mathrm{d}x+\int_{\tfrac i2}^1 (2x-1)\mathrm {d}x
&=\tfrac 12\implies 2\mathcal {I}\geqslant\tfrac 12\implies \mathcal {I}\geqslant\tfrac 14
\end {aligned} $$  Thus,  $\int_0^1 f(x)\mathrm{d}x\geqslant\tfrac 14$ Matching the upper bound  $f(x)=\left|x-\tfrac 12\right|$

We claim that the required minimum possible value is  $\frac{1}{4}$  which can be achieved by the function  $f(x)=|x-\frac{1}{2}|$ .
Now observe that,
\begin{align*}
\int_0^1 f(x) dx&=\lim_{n\to\infty }\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{k=1}^{2n}f(\frac{k}{2n})
&=\lim_{n\to\infty }\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{k=1}^n(f(\frac{k}{2n})+f(\frac{2n+1-k}{2n}))
&\ge\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{2n}(\frac{2n-1}{2n}+\frac{2n-3}{2n}+\dots+\frac{1}{2n})
&=\lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{n^2}{4n^2}=\frac{1}{4}
\end{align*}
So we are done.

