Note that if we prove that  $AEDMB$ ,  $BFDCX$ , and  $EFMX$  are cyclic, the result follows by radical axis theorem. Denote by  $D'$  the point on  $AB$  such that  $AD' = D'C$ .

First we show that  $BFDC$  is cyclic. Note that  $\angle D'BA = \angle D'AC$  since  $AF = FB$  implies that  $\triangle AFB$  is isosceles. Also,  $\angle D'AC = D'CA$  since  $AD' = D'C$  implies that  $AD'C$  is isosceles. Therefore,  $\angle D'BA = \angle D'CA$  and  $D'BCA$  is cyclic. Since  $\triangle ABC$  is right, the center of this circle lies on  $AC$  and the reflection of  $D'$  over  $AC$  lies on the circle. Since  $AC$  is also the angle bisector of  $DAB$  and  $DA = DC$ , the reflection of  $D'$  over  $AC$  is  $D$  itself. Therefore  $BFDC$  is cyclic. 

Now we show that  $AEDMB$  is cyclic.  $\angle ABF = \angle FAD = \angle DCF = \angle DBF$  so  $EA = ED$  implies that  $E$  lies on  $FB$ . Since 
\[\angle EDA = \angle EAD = \angle DAM = \angle MAB = \angle ABF\] $AEDB$  is cyclic. Note that we have used the facts  $EAD$  is isosceles,  $AD$  is the bisector,  $AC$  is the bisector, and  $AFB$  is isosceles in that order. Also, \[\angle BDM = \frac{180^\circ - \angle DMB}{2} = 90^\circ - \angle DCB = 90^\circ - \angle DD'A = \angle BAM,\] so  $M$  lies on this circle. Therefore  $AEDMB$  is cyclic. 

Finally, we show that  $EFMX$  is cyclic. First note that  $AE = ED = DM$  (the latter of which follows by  $\angle EAD = \angle DAM$ ) so  $EDMA$  is an isosceles trapezoid and  $ED||AM$  which implies  $E,X,D$  are collinear. Now  $\angle XEF= \angle DEB = \angle DAB = \angle DCD' = \angle DXD'$ . Thus  $EXMF$  is an isosceles trapezoid and therefore is cyclic.

Now before we are complete, we must show that  $X$  lies on  $BFDC$  to use the radical axis theorem. Note that  $ADD'C$  is a rhombus since  $D$  and  $D'$  are reflections of each other over  $AC$  so  $AD = D'C$ . Also, it is trivial to see that  $AD = FX$  since  $AED \cong FMX$ . In addition,  $\angle DAM= \angle EAD = \angle XFM \implies  FX || AD || D'C$ .  $FXCD'$  is an isosceles trapezoid so  $X$  lies on the circle. We are finished.  $\Box$

