Now we prove that  $9 \mid n$  is a necessary condition. Let  $n = 3k$ , and divide up the grid into  $k^2$  squares with dimensions of  $3 \times 3$ . It suffices to prove the following claim: **<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>**There are an equal quantity of each letter  $I,M,O$  over all the  $k^2$  center cells of each  $3 \times 3$  square. 

*Proof:*The idea is to double count. Consider the count where we take the union of all the center rows and columns of each  $3 \times 3$  square, along with all the diagonals on the grid which have  $0 \pmod 3$  cells lying on the diagonal.

[list]
[*]On one hand, note that there are an equal number of  $I,M,$  and  $O$  in the count, by definition.
[*]On the other hand, this counts all the cells on the grid once, and then the  $k^2$  center cells  $3$  extra times. Since the grid, by definition, has an equal amount of the letters  $I,M,$  and  $O$ , among the  $k^2$  center cells we must have an equal quantity of those letters too.  $\square$

