\begin{align*}
	\gcd(P(n), P(n+1)) &\mid 1 
	\gcd(P(n), P(n+2)) &\mid 7 
	\gcd(P(n), P(n+3)) &\mid 3 
	\gcd(P(n), P(n+4)) &\mid 19.
\end{align*}
Now assume for contradiction  $b \le 5$ . Then any GCD's among  $P(a+1)$ , ...,  $P(a+b)$  must be among  $\{3, 7, 19\}$ . Consider a multi-graph on  $\{a+1, \dots, a+b\}$  where we join two elements with nontrivial GCD and label the edge with the corresponding prime. Then we readily see there is at most one edge each of  $\{3, 7, 19\}$ : id est at most one edge of gap  $2$ ,  $3$ ,  $4$  (and no edges of gap  $1$ ). (By the gap of an edge  $e=\{u,v\}$  we mean  $|u-v|$ .) But one can see (by checking the cases manually) that it's now impossible for every vertex to have nonzero degree, contradiction.

To construct  $b = 6$  we use the Chinese remainder theorem: select  $a$  with
\begin{align*}
	a+1 & \equiv 7 \pmod{19} 
	a+5 & \equiv 11 \pmod{19} 
	a+2 & \equiv 2 \pmod{7} 
	a+4 & \equiv 4 \pmod{7} 
	a+3 & \equiv 1 \pmod{3} 
	a+6 & \equiv 1 \pmod{3}
\end{align*}
which does the trick.

