I was playing around this problem and was able to prove the following stronger version.

<blockquote>For every positive integer  $a,n$  such that  $\gcd(a,n)=1$ , there exists a positive integer  $x$  such that  $x^x\equiv a\pmod n$ </blockquote>

<details><summary>Proof</summary>We will prove the following even stronger claim by induction on  $n$ .
<blockquote>For every positive integer  $a,n$  such that  $\gcd(a,n)=1$ , there exists a positive integer  $x$  such that  $\gcd(x,\mathrm{lcm}(n,\varphi(n)))=1$  and  $x^x\equiv a\pmod n$ </blockquote>
Let  $p$  be the largest prime divisor of  $n$ , set  $n=p^t\cdot m$  where  $p\nmid m$ ,  $d=\mathrm{lcm}(m,\varphi(m))$  and observe that  $\gcd(p,d)=1$ . 

By induction hypothesis, there exists a positive integer  $b$  such that  $\gcd(b,d)=1$  and  $b^b\equiv a\pmod m$ . Let  $c$  be a positive integer such that  $c\equiv b\pmod{d}$  and  $\gcd(c,p-1)=1$ . Note that  $c^c\equiv b^b\equiv a\pmod m$ .

Our strategy is constructing a sequence  $x_1,x_2,...,x_t$  such that  $x_i^{x_i}\equiv a\pmod{p^i\cdot m}$ . To construct  $x_1$ , we need the following lemma.
-----**Lemma :** Let  $p$  be a prime and  $n$  be a positive integer such that  $\gcd(n,p-1)=1$ . Then the set  $\{1^n,2^n,...,p^n\}$  form a complete residue system modulo  $p$ .**Proof :** Obviously  $r^n\equiv 0\pmod p\iff r\equiv 0\pmod p$ . Suppose that  $r^n\equiv s^n\pmod p$  where  $p\nmid r,s$ . Since  $r^{p-1}\equiv s^{p-1}\pmod p$ , by order we have  $r^{\gcd(n,p-1)}\equiv s^{\gcd(n,p-1)}\pmod p$   $\implies r\equiv s\pmod p$  and we are done.  $\blacksquare$ .
-----
Now since  $\gcd(c,p-1)=1$ , we let  $x_1$  be the positive integer such that
\begin{align*}x_1&\equiv c\pmod{(p-1)d}
(x_1)^c &\equiv a\pmod p
\end{align*}
(it exists because CRT). Since  $x_1\equiv c\pmod d$ ,  $(x_1)^{x_1}\equiv c^c\equiv a\pmod m$ . But  $(x_1)^{x_1}\equiv (x_1)^c\equiv a\pmod p$ . Note that from our construction, it is clear that  $\gcd(x_1,p(p-1)d)=1\implies \gcd(x_1,\mathrm{lcm}(\varphi(pm),pm))=1$ .

Now we have to build  $x_2, x_3,..., x_t$  using similar trick to Hensel's Lemma. Suppose we know  $x_i$ , define  $x_{i+1}$  by
\begin{align*}x_{i+1}&\equiv x_i+k\cdot p^i \pmod{p^{i+1}}
x_{i+1} &\equiv x_i \pmod{p^{i}(p-1)d}\end{align*}
for a suitable positive integer  $k$ . There exist such  $k$  if and only if.
\begin{align*}(x_i+k\cdot p^i)^{x_i}\equiv a\pmod{p^{i+1}} &\iff (x_i)^{x_i}+\binom{x_i}{1} (x_i)^{x_i-1}k \cdot p^i \equiv a \pmod{p^{i+1}}
&\iff (x_i)^{x_i}(1+k\cdot p^{i-1})\equiv a\pmod{p^{i+1}}
&\iff \gcd(p^{i-1},p^{i})\mid (x_i)^{x_i}-a\end{align*}
which is true. Finally, note that  $\mathrm{lcm}(\varphi(p^{i+1} m), p^{i+1}m)=\mathrm{lcm}(p^{i}(p-1)\varphi(m),p^{i+1}m)\mid p^{i+1}\cdot \mathrm{lcm}(p-1,d)$  and  $p\nmid x_i$  for every  $i\in\mathbb{N}$ . Hence our  $\gcd$  condition is preserved while building  $x_1,x_2,...$  so we can continue the process of induction and we are done.</details>

