We claim that the area of triangle  $BCP$  is  $\frac{63}{4}$ .

Observe that diagonal  $AC$  lies entirely within the interior of quadrilateral  $ABCD$  by its convexity. Then, notice that since  $AC$  is a diagonal of quadrilateral  $ABCD$ , point  $P$  on segment  $CD$  and vertex  $B$  lie in distinct half-planes determined by the line  $AC$ . Furthermore, segment  $BP$  must also be entirely within the interior of quadrilateral  $ABCD$  by its convexity. So, segments  $BP$  and  $AC$  intersect, and they intersect inside quadrilateral  $ABCD$ . We call their intersection  $X$ .

We are given  $\triangle ABP\cong\triangle ACD$ , so we have  $AB=AC=20$  and  $AP=AD=16$ . This congruence also gives   $$ \angle PAB=\angle DAC \implies \angle PAC+\angle CAB=\angle DAP+\angle PAC \implies \angle CAB =\angle DAP. $$   So by SAS similarity,  $\triangle CAB\sim\triangle DAP$ . It follows that   $$ \left(\frac{20}{16}\right)^2=\frac{[CAB]}{[DAP]}=\left(\frac{20}{16}\right)^2=\frac{[ABX]+[XBC]}{28}\implies \frac{175}{4}=[ABX]+[XBC]. $$  Lastly, we also have from the congruence that   $$ [ABP]=[ACD]\implies [AXP]+[ABX]=[DAP]+[AXP]+[CPX]\implies [ABX]-28=[CPX]. $$   Therefore, we can conclude that  $$ [BCP]=[CPX]+[XBC]=([ABX]+[XBC])-28=\frac{175}{4}-28=\frac{63}{4}. $$

Expanding on skrublord's solution:

We generalize to the first  $2k$  positive integers,  $k$  of which are circled.

Every score can be written as  $(\pm 1 \pm 2 \pm 3 \pm \dots \pm 2k)^2$ , where half of the signs are positive (the circled numbers) and half are negative (the uncircled numbers). In other words, if we define  $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{2k}$  by  $$ a_i = \bigg\{
    \begin{array}{cl}
    1 & \text{if}\ i \text{ is circled} 
    -1 & \text{otherwise},
    \end{array} $$  the score is  $\bigg(\sum_{i = 1}^{2k} a_i i \bigg)^2$ , which can be expanded as
\begin{align}
    \bigg(\sum_{i = 1}^{2k} a_i^2 i^2\bigg) + 2\bigg(\sum_{i = 2}^{2k}\sum_{j = 1}^{i-1} a_i a_j i j\bigg).
\end{align}
The left summand is constant, equal to  $\bigg(\sum_{i = 1}^{2k} i^2\bigg) = \frac{2k(2k + 1)(4k + 1)}{6}$ . In the right summand,  $a_i a_j$  will be  $1$  if  $a_i = a_j$  (i.e., when  $i$  and  $j$  are either both circled or both uncircled), and  $-1$  otherwise. Out of all  $\textstyle\binom{2k}{k}$  possible sets of circled numbers, there are  $\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2}$  sets where  $i$  and  $j$  are both circled (since we are choosing  $k - 2$  of the remaining numbers to circle), and likewise there are  $\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2}$  sets where  $i$  and  $j$  are both uncircled. Thus, summing over all possibilities, the term  $ij$  appears  $2\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2}$  times, and the term  $-ij$  appears  $\textstyle\binom{2k}{k} - 2\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2}$  times. Adding these occurrences together yields  $$ \Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)ij, $$  so the sum of the values of  $\sum_{i = 2}^{2k}\sum_{j = 1}^{i-1} a_i a_j i j$  over all sets of circled numbers is
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i = 2}^{2k}\sum_{j = 1}^{i-1} \Big(\big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\big)ij\Big)
    &= \Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\displaystyle\sum_{i = 2}^{2k}\sum_{j = 1}^{i-1} ij 
    &= \Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\displaystyle\sum_{i = 2}^{2k} \frac{i^2(i - 1)}{2} 
    &= \tfrac{1}{2}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\bigg(\displaystyle\sum_{i = 2}^{2k} i^3 - \sum_{i = 2}^{2k} i^2\bigg) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{2}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\Big(\frac{(2k)^2(2k + 1)^2}{4} - 1 - \frac{2k(2k + 1)(4k + 1)}{6} + 1\Big) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{2}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(\frac{3k(2k + 1) - (4k + 1)}{3}\Big) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(6k^2 - k  - 1\Big) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} - \textstyle\binom{2k}{k}\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(2k - 1\Big)\Big(3k + 1\Big).
\end{align*}
Dividing by the total number of sets,  $\textstyle\binom{2k}{k}$ , we find that the average value is
\begin{align*}
    \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(4\textstyle\binom{2k - 2}{k - 2} / \textstyle\binom{2k}{k} - 1\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(2k - 1\Big)\Big(3k + 1\Big)
    &= \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(4\textstyle\cdot\frac{(2k - 2)!}{(k - 2)!k!}\cdot\frac{k!k!}{(2k)!} - 1\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(2k - 1\Big)\Big(3k + 1\Big) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(4\textstyle\cdot\frac{k(k-1)}{2k(2k-1)} - 1\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(2k - 1\Big)\Big(3k + 1\Big) 
    &= \tfrac{1}{6}\Big(\textstyle\frac{1}{2k-1}\Big)\Big(k\Big)\Big(2k + 1\Big)\Big(2k - 1\Big)\Big(3k + 1\Big) 
    &= \frac{k(2k+1)(3k+1)}{6}.
\end{align*}
Finally, we plug this into  $(1)$  to find that the average score is  $$ \frac{2k(2k + 1)(4k + 1)}{6} - 2\Big(\frac{k(2k+1)(3k+1)}{6}\Big) = \frac{k^2(2k + 1)}{3}. $$  In our case,  $k = 100$ , so the average score is  $\frac{100^2 \cdot 201}{3} = \boxed{670,\!000}$ .

