Consider a  $(2m-1)\times(2n-1)$  rectangular region, where  $m$  and  $n$  are integers such that  $m,n\ge 4.$  The region is to be tiled using tiles of the two types shown:
\[
\begin{picture}(140,40)

\put(0,0){\line(0,1){40}}
\put(0,0){\line(1,0){20}}
\put(0,40){\line(1,0){40}}
\put(20,0){\line(0,1){20}}
\put(20,20){\line(1,0){20}}
\put(40,20){\line(0,1){20}}
\multiput(0,20)(5,0){4}{\line(1,0){3}}
\multiput(20,20)(0,5){4}{\line(0,1){3}}

\put(80,0){\line(1,0){40}}
\put(120,0){\line(0,1){20}}
\put(120,20){\line(1,0){20}}
\put(140,20){\line(0,1){20}}
\put(80,0){\line(0,1){20}}
\put(80,20){\line(1,0){20}}
\put(100,20){\line(0,1){20}}
\put(100,40){\line(1,0){40}}
\multiput(100,0)(0,5){4}{\line(0,1){3}}
\multiput(100,20)(5,0){4}{\line(1,0){3}}
\multiput(120,20)(0,5){4}{\line(0,1){3}}

\end{picture}
\]
(The dotted lines divide the tiles into  $1\times 1$  squares.) The tiles may be rotated and reflected, as long as their sides are parallel to the sides of the rectangular region. They must all fit within the region, and they must cover it completely without overlapping.

What is the minimum number of tiles required to tile the region?

solution_md:
My version, similar to v_enhance but slightly different. Also, some extra details that aren't needed, and lots of figures with meaningful colors.

We label our two possible tile types the three-square  $\Gamma$  and the four-square  $Z$ .
Clearly, among all possible tilings of a lattice figure, the one that uses the fewest total tiles is the one that uses the most  $Z$ -tiles and the fewest  $\Gamma$ -tiles.

Now, color each square in our rectangle by the parity of its two possible coordinates, for four colors. (Let the squares be at  $(a,b)$  for  $1\le a\le 2m-1$  and  $1\le b\le 2n-1$ . Yellow for both  $x$  and  $y$  odd, green for  $x$  even and  $y$  odd, blue for  $x$  odd and  $y$  even, red for  $x$  and  $y$  both even.)

[asy]
unitsize(0.8cm);
pen a = paleyellow;
pen b = palegreen;
pen c = paleblue;
pen d = palered;
pen[]colors = {a,b,c,d};

real x,y;
for (int i=0; i<7; ++i) {
 x= i;
 for (int j=0; j<7; ++j) {
  y=j;
  filldraw(shift(x,y)*unitsquare,colors[i%2 + 2*(j%2)],black);
 }
}
[/asy]



There are  $mn$  yellow squares,  $(m-1)n$  green squares,  $m(n-1)$  blue squares, and  $(m-1)(n-1)$  red squares in the rectangle.
Now, we look at the tiles. Each  $Z$  tile, no matter its position or orientation, has one square of each color in it. The variation between the colors must therefore come from the  $\Gamma$  tiles, each of which has one square of three colors and omits the fourth. Labeling each  $\Gamma$ -tile with the omitted color, a successful tiling has  $mn-m(n-1)=m$  more blue  $\Gamma$ -tiles than yellow  $\Gamma$ -tiles,  $mn-(m-1)n=n$  more green  $\Gamma$ -tiles than yellow  $\Gamma$ -tiles, and  $mn-(m-1)(n-1)=m+n-1$  more red  $\Gamma$ -tiles than yellow  $\Gamma$ -tiles.
The number of yellow  $\Gamma$ -tiles must be at least zero, so the rectangle's tiling has at least  $m+n+(m+n-1)=2m+2n-1$   $\Gamma$ -tiles in it. If there are  $G$   $\Gamma$ -tiles among  $T$  total tiles, then, counting squares,  $(2m-1)(2n-1)=3G+4(T-G)=4T-G$ . Thus  $2m+2n-1\le G\le 4T-(2m-1)(2n-1)=4T-4mn+2m+2n-1$ . Solving for  $T$ ,  $T\ge mn$ .

So, why was there that  $m,n\ge 4$  restriction in the problem? Because without it, we might not be able to tile the rectangle at all. In order for a tiling of the rectangle to be possible, we need  $mn\ge 2m+2n-1$ , or equivalently  $(m-2)(n-2)\ge 3$ . There are no tilings of  $3\times (2n-1)$ ,  $5\times 5$ , or  $5\times 7$  rectangles, or reflections thereof.

First, the base cases:

[asy]
