These use the maximum possible numbers of  $Z$ -tiles;  $1$  in the first case and  $0$  in the second. I found them by trial and only a little error; they're not remotely close to unique. The coloring helps a lot, as we can see which tile positions to avoid. Just try, backtrack a bit if you get stuck, and you'll find something.

If we have a tiling of a  $(2m-1)\times (2n-1)$  rectangle in  $mn$  tiles, we can add on a  $(2m-1)\times 2$  block in  $m$  tiles, for  $(2m-1)(2n+1)$  in  $m(n+1)$  tiles, as follows:

[asy]
unitsize(0.8cm);
pen b = palegreen;
pen d = palered;
pen e = yellow;
filldraw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--cycle,d,black);
filldraw((2,0)--(4,0)--(4,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(1,2)--(1,1)--(2,1)--cycle,e,black);
filldraw((4,0)--(6,0)--(6,1)--(5,1)--(5,2)--(3,2)--(3,1)--(4,1)--cycle,e,black);
filldraw((6,0)--(7,0)--(7,2)--(5,2)--(5,1)--(6,1)--cycle,b,black);
[/asy]

Increasing or decreasing  $m$  just means increasing or decreasing the number of  $Z$ -tiles between the two  $\Gamma$ -tile caps.

A vertical  $(2n-1)\times 2$  can be done in the same way, and we really don't need a new figure. This allows us to increment either  $m$  or  $n$ , eventually reaching everything greater than what we started with.

By induction on  $m$  and  $n$ , a  $(2m-1)\times (2n-1)$  rectangle can be tiled in  $mn$  total tiles of these types for all  $m,n\ge 4$  and also for  $m=3,n\ge 5$  and  $m\ge 5,n=3$ .

candidates:
The answer is  $mn$ .

To see that at least  $mn$  tiles are needed, index the rows and columns by  $(i,j)$  with  $1 \le i \le 2m-1$  and  $1 \le j \le 2n-1$ .  Then there are  $mn$  squares in which  $i$  and  $j$  are odd and each tile covers at most one such square as desired.  

Now we need to show this bound is tight.  It suffices to prove that a  $2 \times (2k-1)$  rectangle may be tiled by  $k$  tiles, and that a  $7 \times 7$  square may be tiled by  $16$  tiles, since the entire  $(2m-1) \times (2n-1)$  rectangle may be easily dissected into such regions.  

The former construction is easy: since one can take  $k-2$  S-dominoes and  $2$  L-dominoes.

The latter construction is harder (and most of the difficulty of the problem).  A construction is exhibited below.

[asy]
size(6cm);

pen a = lightgreen;
pen b = lightred;
pen c = lightblue;
pen d = black;

pen[] colors = {
a,a,c,c,a,a,b,
a,b,d,c,a,b,b,
b,b,d,d,c,d,d,
a,a,b,d,c,c,d,
a,b,b,a,a,b,b,
c,c,d,a,c,d,b,
c,d,d,c,c,d,d
};

real x, y;
for (int i=0; i<49; ++i) {
x = i % 7;
y = floor(i/7);
filldraw(shift(x,y)*unitsquare, white+colors[i], black);
}
[/asy]

First, let me say something about finding a construction. You can find one fairly easily if you take advantage of symmetry. If there are  $x$  triominos and  $y$  tetrominos, we know that  $3x+4y=49$  and  $x+y=16$ , hence  $(x,y)=(15,1)$ . This suggests that to find a symmetrical arrangement, we should think of the tetromino as a  $1\times 1$  tile attached to a triomino. Then as the center square is the only fixed point under rotations, we anchor the tetromino (and it's  $1\times 1$  square) in the center. From here, we rotate the false triomino to place three other triominos, and it's relatively easy to first build out some wings, and then fill out the remaining  $2\times 3$  squares as shown below.
[asy]
draw((0,0)--(7,0)--(7,7)--(0,7)--cycle);
draw((0,2)--(3,2)--(3,0));
draw((5,0)--(5,3)--(7,3));
draw((7,5)--(4,5)--(4,7));
draw((2,7)--(2,4)--(0,4));
draw((2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2));
draw((7,2)--(6,2)--(6,1)--(5,1));
draw((5,7)--(5,6)--(6,6)--(6,5));
draw((2,6)--(1,6)--(1,5)--(0,5));
draw((3,2)--(4,2)--(4,1)--(5,1));
draw((5,3)--(5,4)--(6,4)--(6,5));
draw((4,5)--(3,5)--(3,6)--(2,6));
draw((2,4)--(2,3)--(1,3)--(1,2));
draw((3,2)--(3,4)--(4,4)--(4,3)--(5,3));
draw((4,4)--(4,5)^^(3,4)--(2,4));
draw((3,3)--(4,3),dotted);
draw((3,2)--(3,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(7,3)--(7,5)--(4,5)--(4,7)--(2,7)--(2,4)--(0,4)--(0,2)--cycle,linewidth(2));
[/asy]

My proof used a more complicated coloring approach, which turned out to be a similar argument in the end, but it provides a bit of variety.

Suppose that we color the grid using horizontal stripes as shown below.
[asy]
for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {fill((0,2*i)--(7,2*i)--(7,2*i+1)--(0,2*i+1)--cycle,mediumgray);}
for (int i=0;i<8;++i) {draw((0,i)--(7,i));draw((i,0)--(i,7));}
[/asy]
Suppose that the tiling contains of  $x$  triomino tiles and  $y$  tetromino tiles. Then clearly  $3x+4y=(2m-1)(2n-1)$ , and we wish to minimize the value of  $x+y$ . Note that each tetromino tile will cover two black squares and two white squares. However, there are  $2n-1$  extra black squares in our coloring. This differential must therefore be caused by the triominos.

Each triomino can either cover two black squares and one white square (we call this a black triomino), or two white squares and one black square (a white triomino). In this coloring of striped rows, we let  $b_r$  be the number of black triominos and  $w_r$  be the number of white triominos. Then clearly 
\[b_r+w_r=x.\tag{1}\]
Also, we note that as there are  $2n-1$  extra black squares in the coloring, then
\[b_r-w_r=(2n-1).\tag{2}\]
If we instead use a coloring where the columns are striped, we let  $b_c$  be the number of black triominos and  $w_c$  be the number of white triominos. Similar to above, we find that
\begin{align*}
b_c+w_c&=x\tag{3}
b_c-w_c&=(2m-1).\tag{4}
\end{align*}
We now have a couple of systems of equations, and if we solve (1) and (2) for  $w_r$ , and (3) and (4) for  $w_c$ , we find
\begin{align*}
w_r&=\frac12(x-(2n-1))\tag{5}
w_c&=\frac12(x-(2m-1)).\tag{6}
\end{align*}
Next, we overlay the two colorings as shown below (this is similar to the odd coordinates).
[asy]
for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {fill((0,2*i)--(7,2*i)--(7,2*i+1)--(0,2*i+1)--cycle,mediumgray);}
for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {fill((2*i,0)--(2*i,7)--(2*i+1,7)--(2*i+1,0)--cycle,mediumgray);}
for (int i=0;i<8;++i) {draw((0,i)--(7,i));draw((i,0)--(i,7));}
[/asy]
In this coloring, it is clear that each of the  $(m-1)(n-1)$  white squares will be covered by exactly one tile. On the other hand, every tetromino must cover exactly one white square. Suppose that  $z$  is the number of triominos that cover a white square. Then we can count the number of white squares in two ways, finding
\[y+z=(m-1)(n-1).\tag{7}\]
But note that every one of the  $z$  triominos that covers a white square will be a white triomino in at least one of the the row/column striped colorings, but possibly both. In fact, it will only be a white triomino in both colorings if its corner square is placed over a white square in the overlay tiling (call this a "doubly white triomino). Therefore,  $z\le w_r+w_c$ , with equality if and only if there are no doubly white triominos.  Substituting (5) and (6) into this inequality, we find
\[z\le x-m-n+1.\tag{8}\]
Substituting (8) into (7), we conclude that
\[y+x-m-n+1\ge mn-m-n+1,\]
hence  $x+y\ge mn$ , with equality if and only if there are no doubly white triominos.

From here, it's easy to see that the general construction (in the same manner of v_Enhance and jmerry) has no doubly white triominos, so it must achieve equality. Thus the minimum value is indeed  $mn$ .

solution_md:
First, I prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** If an element of  $G$  can be represented in the form   $$ g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_r} h^{n_r} $$   for some  $r>|G|,$  then it is representable in the form   $$ g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_{r'}} h^{n_{r'}} $$   for some  $1 \le r' \le |G|.$  
*Proof.* Suppose for the sake of contradiction that some element  $x$  of  $G$  can be expressed in the form   $$ g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_r} h^{n_r} $$   where  $r>|G|,$  and  $r$  is minimal. However, if we consider the following elements of  $G:$    $$ \{g^{m_1}h^{n_1}, g^{m_1}h^{n_1}g^{m_2}h^{n_2}, \ldots, g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_r} h^{n_r}\}, $$   then since  $r>|G|,$  we know that some two of them must be the same, i.e.   $$ g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_p} h^{n_p}=g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_q} h^{n_q} $$   for some  $1 \le p<q \le r.$  However, this means that this element of  $G$  is also equal to   $$ g^{m_1} h^{n_1} g^{m_2} h^{n_2} \dots g^{m_p} h^{n_p}g^{m_{q+1}}h^{n_{q+1}}\dots g^{m_r}h^{n_r}, $$   and we have
\begin{align*}
    p+(r-q) &<p+(r-p) 
    &= r,
\end{align*}
contradicting the minimality of  $r.$  

Hence, our original supposition must have been false, concluding the proof of this lemma.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, let us greedily determine which elements of  $G$  are representable in the given form. Suppose that the subgroup  $S$  of  $G$  contains all elements of  $G$  representable in the given form. Then, we know that applying  $gh$  to all elements of  $S$  must give us  $S$  back and applying  $gh^{-1}$  to all elements of  $S$  must also give us  $S$  back. This implies that applying any combination of  $gh$  and  $g^{-1}h$  to  $S$  will give us  $S$  back, so we know that
\begin{align*}
    S &= S(gh)(g^{-1}h)^{-1} 
    &= Sghh^{-1}g 
    &= Sg^2.
\end{align*}
Therefore,  $\text{ord}(g) \mid 2,$  so the fact that it is odd implies that   $$ \text{ord}(g)=1 \implies Sg=S. $$   This further tells us that   $$ S=Sgh \implies S=Sh. $$   However, this means that  $S$  is generated by the two elements  $g$  and  $h,$  so  $S=G,$  concluding the proof.  $\square$

