stem_md:
Given positive integers  $m$  and  $n \ge m$ , determine the largest number of dominoes ( $1\times2$  or  $2 \times 1$  rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with  $m$  rows and  $2n$  columns consisting of cells ( $1 \times 1$ squares) so that:
(i) each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;
(ii) no two dominoes overlap;
(iii) no two form a  $2 \times 2$  square; and
(iv) the bottom row of the board is completely covered by  $n$  dominoes.

candidates:
**<span style="color:blue">Part (a):**</span> The sequence  $P(n) = n^3-n^2+n$  works. Indeed, suppose that  $y^2 = n^3-n^2+n$  for some integer  $|n| > 1$ . Then  $n = k^2$  must be a perfect square, from where  $n^4-n^2+1$  must be a perfect square. For  $|n| > 1$ , we have  $\left(n^2-1\right)^2 < n^4-n^2+1 < n^4$  is between two perfect squares, so no such  $n$  can exist.**<span style="color:blue">Part (b):**</span> The only possible value is zero. Consider the elliptic curve  $\mathcal E$  given by \[y^2 = x^3 + bx^2+cx+d.\] By assumption, the lattice points  $\left(0, \sqrt d\right)$ ,  $\left(1, \sqrt{1+b+c+d}\right)$ , and  $\left(1, -\sqrt{1+b+c+d}\right)$  lie on the curve.

In particular, the two lines given by 
\begin{align*}
\ell_1: y = \left(\sqrt{1+b+c+d} - \sqrt d\right) x + \sqrt d 
\ell_2: y = -\left(\sqrt{1+b+c+d} + \sqrt d\right) x + \sqrt d
\end{align*}
must intersect  $\mathcal E$  again at lattice points by Vieta's formulas. The  $x$ -coordinates of these lattice points work out to be 
\begin{align*}
x_1 &= c+2d - 2\sqrt d \sqrt{b+c+d+1} \in \{0, 1\} 
x_2 &= c+2d + 2\sqrt d\sqrt{b+c+d+1} \in \{0, 1\}.
\end{align*}
The  $x$ -coordinates of these points must be in  $\{0, 1\}$  as no other lattice points lie on  $\mathcal E$  by assumption. On the other hand, if  $\sqrt{d(b+c+d+1)} \geq 1$ , then  $x_2-x_1 \geq 2$ , which is absurd. So  $a_0a_1 = \sqrt{d(b+c+d+1)} = 0$  is forced, as needed.

