We will denote the floor of  $x$  as  $[x]$ . Note that each number  $d$  in  $1, \ldots, m$  appears  $\left [ \frac{m}{d} \right ]$  times in  $\sum_{i=1}^m f(i)$ . That means we have 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^m f(i) &= \sum_{d=1}^m \frac{1}{1+d} \left [ \frac{m}{d} \right ] 
&\leq \sum_{d=1}^m \frac{1}{1+d} \frac{m}{d}
&=\sum_{d=1}^m \frac{m}{d} - \frac{m}{1+d} 
&=m-\frac{m}{m+1} < m 
\end{align*}

Let  $a_n\leq (n+c)^2$  for all positive integers  $n$  where  $c\in \mathbb{Z}^+$ . Let  $f(n)=\frac{a_1+\dots+a_n}{n^2}$  which is a positive integer for each  $n$ . We will work on sufficiently large  $n$ .
\[f(n)+2=\frac{a_1+\dots+a_n}{n^2}+2>\frac{a_1+\dots+a_n}{(n+1)^2}+\frac{(n+c)^2}{(n+1)^2}>\frac{a_1+\dots+a_{n+1}}{(n+1)^2}=f(n+1)\]
Thus,  $f(n)+1\geq f(n+1)$ . We have  $n^2+2n+1|n^2f(n)+a_{n+1}$  or  $(n+1)^2|a_{n+1}-(2n+1)f(n)$ . Notice that  $\frac{|a_{n+1}-(2n+1)f(n)|}{(n+1)^2}<3$  since  $f(n)-n$  is bounded above and  $a_{n+1}\leq (n+c+1)^2<2(n+1)^2$ , also it's an integer.  $$ a_{n+1} =
\begin{cases}
(2n+1)f(n) & f(n+1)=f(n)  \text{X} 
(2n+1)f(n)+(n+1)^2 & f(n+1)=f(n)+1  \text{  Y} 
(2n+1)f(n)-(n+1)^2 & f(n+1)=f(n)-1  \text{  Z} 
(2n+1)f(n)-2(n+1)^2 & f(n+1)=f(n)-2  \text{  T}
\end{cases} $$   $a_{n+1}$  is determined by one of  $X,Y,Z,T$  moves.**<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>**  $T$  cannot be made infinitely many times.**<span style="color:#f00">Proof:</span>** Suppose that move  $T$  is made  $t$  times in the first  $m$  moves where  $a_{m+1}$  is determined by  $T$ . Pick  $t$  sufficiently large. Then,  $f(m)\leq \ell+(m-t)-2t<m-t$  for some constant  $\ell$  however,  $m-t>f(m)\geq \frac{2(m+1)^2}{2m+1}>m$  which is impossible.**<span style="color:#00f">Claim: </span>** $Y$  cannot be made infinitely many times.**<span style="color:#f00">Proof:</span>** Assume contrary. If  $Y$  is made in  $m$ th move, then  $(2m+1)f(m)+(m+1)^2=a_{m+1}\leq (m+1+c)^2$  hence  $(2m+1)f(m)\leq c(2m+c+2)$  or  $f(m)\leq \frac{c(2m+c+2)}{2m+1}<c+1$  thus,  $f(m)\leq c$  and this implies  $Z$  must be made infinitely many times, too because it's the only move which can be made infinitely many times, decreasing  $f$ . If  $Z$  is made in  $r$ th move, then  $(2r+1)f(r)\geq(r+1)^2$  or  $f(r)\geq \frac{r^2+2r+1}{2r+1}>\frac{r}{2}$ , pick  $r$  sufficiently large. Since  $Y$  must be made at some point after  $r$  and  $f$  changes by at most  $1$ , it must be in interval  $[c+2025,\frac{r-2025}{2}]$  for some  $f(s)$ . But  $f(s)>c$  and  $f(s)<\frac{s}{2}$  gives that  $a_{s+1}$  cannot be determined by  $Y$  or  $Z$  thus, the  $XYZ$  sequence only contains  $X$  after some point which gives a contradiction.

For sufficiently large  $n$ , there is either  $X$  or  $Z$  at  $n$ th move hence  $f$  becomes nonincreasing. Thus, move  $Z$  cannot be made infinitely many times and move  $X$  must be made after some point. Thus,  $a_{n+1}=(2n+1)f(n)$  and  $f(n+1)=f(n)$  for sufficiently large  $n$ . This yields  $a_{n+1}=(2n+1)d$  for some constant  $d$  for sufficiently large  $n$ . Hence  $a_{n+1}-a_n=2d$  for  $n\gg $  as desired. $\blacksquare$

