Here, we will prove the following $\textbf{\textit{Lemma:}}$  All small triangles must have an area of  $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$ .  $\textbf{\textit{Proof:}}$  Notice that we cannot construct triangles that are any smaller from the lattice points; indeed,  $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$  is the area of the smallest possible triangle with vertcies on the triangular grid. Also, since the large triangle has an area of  $\frac{\sqrt{3} \cdot n^2}{4} = n^2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$ , it must be constructed by exactly  $n^2$  triangles with an area of  $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$  each. 
A direct corollary of the  $\textbf{\textit{lemma}}$  is that all row lines (i.e. lines between two neighbours in the same "row", formally defined as grouping by perpendicular distance from points to a pre-chosen side of the big triangle) must all be drawn - since each small triangle ought to include exactly one, and row lines from all rows but the last are shared between two triangles - or simply due to double counting performed on the number of row lines in a grid with  $n$  rows:   $$ n+2 \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i\right)=n^2. $$    $$ \color{magenta} \bigstar\color{magenta} \boxed{\textbf{Inductive solution.}}\color{magenta} \bigstar $$  With that in mind, we will prove that every row contains at least one equilateral triangle, implying the problem condition. <details><summary>*</summary>Notice that, formally, the point on top is of row " $0$ ", and we consider  $n$  numbered rows.</details>
We perform induction - the base case is clear (the tippity top of the triangle), so we assume that the statement is true for the space between the  $k-1-$ th and  $k-$ th row. Let us now consider that between the  $k-$ th and the  $k+1-$ th:
[asy]
size(200);
real h = sqrt(3)/2;
real r = 0.12;

