It is well known that  $T'$ ,  $I$ , and  $G$  are collinear and that  $AK$  and  $AT'$  are isogonal with respect to  $\angle A$ . Note that when we reflect  $A$ ,  $L$ ,  $J$ , and  $K$  over  $FG$ , we get  $H$ ,  $L$ ,  $D$ , and  $T'$ , so those four points are collinear. Since  $FG$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $AH$ ,  $GA=GH$ , so  $G$  is the midpoint of arc  $AH$  and  $\angle GTA=\angle GTH$ . Hence,  $\angle DT'F=90^{\circ}-\angle GT'D=90^{\circ}-\angle GT'H$  and  $\angle DEM=180^{\circ}-\angle FEM=180^{\circ}-(90^{\circ}-\angle EFM)=90^{\circ}+\angle AFG=90^{\circ}+\angle GT'H$ , so  $DEFT'$  is cyclic. Thus,  $T'\equiv T$ .

