Let  $X = \overline{AG} \cap \overline{BC}$ . We begin with the following claim:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $XGQN$  is cyclic.

*Proof.* Observe that the line  $GN$  meets  $(ABC)$  again at the  $A$ -antipode, which is well-known by the Orthocenter Reflection Lemma. Hence,  $\overline{NG} \perp \overline{AX}$ , and it follows that  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle AXN$ . The claim follows by the orthic triangle.  $\blacksquare$ Next, we claim that  $\overline{PA}$  is tangent to  $\gamma$ . This follows because  $P$  lies on  $\overline{OM}$ , so  $\overline{PM}$  bisects  $\overline{AG}$ , and that  $\overline{PG}$  is tangent to  $\gamma$ . As a conseqeunce, quadrilateral  $APGM$  is cyclic.

From here, we present two approaches.**<span style="color:blue">Approach 1, with Radical Axis**</span> Denote by  $\omega$  the nine-point circle of  $(ABC)$ . Consider the three circles  $(APGM)$ ,  $\omega$  and  $(BMC)$ . They meet at the midpoint of altitude  $M$ ; we claim that  $X$  also lies on their radical axis.

To observe this, just notice that  $X$  has equal power to  $(APGM), \omega, (AEHF)$  and  $(ABC)$  by radical axis, but it also lies on  $\overline{BM}$ , implying that  $X$  has equal power to  $(APGM), \omega$  and  $(BMC)$ . 

Now, this implies that these three circles are coaxial, so they share another common point. Call this point  $T$ . Observe that  $T$  lies on  $\overline{XM}$ , so  $\measuredangle XTN = \measuredangle MTN = 90^{\circ}$  because  $\overline{MN}$  is a diameter of  $\omega$ . Hence,  $T$  lies on both  $(QGN)$  and  $(BMC)$ .

To finish, observe that  $T$  also lies on  $(AGM)$ . Then  $\measuredangle PTM = \measuredangle NTM = 90^{\circ}$ , and it follows  $T, P, N$  are collinear. Thus,  $(QGN)$  and  $(BMC)$  intersect on  $\overline{PN}$ , as required.  $\square$ **<span style="color:blue">Approach 2, with Spiral Similarity**</span> The key lies in the following claim:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $\overline{PN}$  and  $\overline{XM}$  meet at the second intersection of  $(APGM)$  and  $(XGQN)$ .

*Proof.* The key is that because  $\measuredangle XGN = \measuredangle PGM = 90^{\circ}$ , while   $$ \measuredangle GMP = \measuredangle GAP = \measuredangle NXG $$   because  $\overline{AP} \parallel \overline{BC}$  due to our tangency. Now, a spiral similarity at  $G$  sends  $GMP$  to  $GXN$ , so a spiral similarity at  $G$  also sends  $GMX$  to  $GPN$ . The claim follows by the Spiral Similarity Lemma.  $\blacksquare$ Next,  $X$  is the radical center of  $(APGM), (ABC)$  and  $(BMC)$ , so  $(APGM)$  and  $(BMC)$  intersect again at a point on  $\overline{XM}$ . However, that point is precisely  $T$  -- thus,  $T$  lies on  $(BMC)$ , and we can finish in a similar manner as the first approach.  $\square$

