Suppose there exists a configuration of walls for which the bulldozer hits both sides of each. Define the ``down-area'' and ``right-area'' of a wall  $W$  (the wall is red) to be all points directly down and right to the wall:
[asy]
unitsize(0.75cm);
draw((0,0)--(1,1), red);
draw((0,-2)--(0,0), dotted,BeginArrow);
draw((1,-2)--(1,1),dotted,BeginArrow);
label("down-area",(2,-2.5),dir(180));

int x=4;
draw((x+0,0)--(x+1,1), red);
draw((x+0,0)--(x+3,0), dotted,EndArrow);
draw((x+1,1)--(x+3,1),dotted,EndArrow);
label("right-area",(6.8,-1),dir(180));
[/asy]
Similarly define the ``left-area'' and the ``up-area''. Call a wall  $W'$  *below* a wall  $W$  if there exists a point of  $W'$  below a point of  $W$  (smaller y-coordinate).
----------
<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 1:**</span> We can assume that every wall  $W$  has a wall below it. 

<span style="color:#00f">*Proof:*</span> Consider the two directions that the bulldozer starts and ends with (these could be the same). Define the *correct* direction to be an arbitrary one of the four directions not one of these two. (For example, if the start and end directions are up and right, then a correct direction is down.) WLOG suppose a correct direction is down. 

Fix a wall  $W$ . There must exist a point of at least one other wall inside the down-area of  $W$ , since the bulldozer either came in or will go out by hitting  $W$ , and in one of these, the bulldozer passed through the down-area, which means another point of a wall is in the down-area. Note that this holds for *every* wall  $W$  since a correct direction is down, which means even on the first and last walls, there is a point of another wall in its down-area.  $\blacksquare$ -------------
<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 2:**</span> Suppose a wall  $W_1$  is below a wall  $W_2$ . Then the down-area of of  $W_2$  does not have any points in common with the up-area of  $W_1$ . 

<span style="color:#00f">*Proof:*</span> This is intuitively clear, and a proof is also easy. Below,  $W_1$  is blue and  $W_1$  is red. 
[asy]
unitsize(1.25cm);
draw((0,0)--(1,1), red);
draw((0,-2)--(0,0), dotted,BeginArrow);
draw((1,-2)--(1,1),dotted,BeginArrow);
draw((0,3.2)--(0,0), dotted,BeginArrow);
draw((1,3.2)--(1,1),dotted,BeginArrow);

pair P = (0.7,-1); 
dot(P);
label(" $P$ ",P,dir(140));
draw((0.5,-2-1/3)--(1.3,3), blue);
draw((0.5,-7/3)--(0.5,-3),dotted,EndArrow);
draw((1.3,3)--(1.3,-3),dotted,EndArrow);
[/asy]
Consider a point  $P$  in the down-area of the red that is a point of the blue line. Then the blue line is a line passing through  $P$ , and can rotate to at most the position shown above (or the left side, in which case the argument is isomorphic). The down-area of the blue line cannot intersect the up-area of the red line's up-area, even past the right vertex of the red line, as shown.  $\blacksquare$ -------------
Iteratively apply Claim 1 to create a chain of walls, one each below than the next. Since there are only finitely many walls, this chain must be a cycle. We claim there cannot be such a cycle of walls. Suppose  $W_1\to W_2\to \cdots \to  W_n\to W_1$  is a cycle of walls, each below the next. By Claim 2, the down-area of  $W_2$  does not intersect the up-area of  $W_1$ . Also, the down-area of  $W_3$  does not intersect the up-area of  $W_2$ , and hence also bounded not intersecting the up-area of  $W_1$ , since then  $W_3$  would intersect  $W_2$ . Continuing with this logic, the down-area of  $W$  does not intersect the up-area of  $W_1$  for any  $W$  in the chain. This contradicts that the chain cycles back to  $W_1$ .

<details><summary>Remarks</summary>The main idea is that we get by the nature of the trajectory that for each wall, its (WLOG) down-area intersects with another wall. From here, it's natural to consider just applying this claim over and over to get a chain, which must be a cycle since finitely many. From here, we have a very strong foothold, and it is intuitively obvious we cannot have a cycle, and the logic of Claim 2 and the finish are natural to finish.</details>

