Let  $\Delta XYZ$  be the medial triangle of  $\Delta DEF$  where  $\{X,Y,Z\}\in\{\overline{EF},\overline{FD},\overline{DE}\}$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 1:- </span>**    $\{X\}\in$  Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .
Consider an Inversion  $\Psi$  around  $\odot(I)$ . Clearly  $\{\Psi(X),\Psi(Y),\Psi(Z)\}=\{A,B,C\}\implies\Psi(\odot(AIC))=\overline{XZ}$ . So,  $\Psi(\odot(I)\cap\odot(AIC))=\odot(I)\cap\overline{XZ}\implies\{B_1,B_2\}\in\overline{XZ}$ . Similarly  $\{C_1,C_2\}\in\overline{XY}$ . So, taking pairwise radical axis and applying radical axis theorem on  $\odot(BB_1B_2),\odot(I),\odot(CC_1C_2)$  we get that  $\overline{B_1B_2}\cap\overline{C_1C_2}=\{X\}\in$  Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 2:-</span>**   $\overline{BZ}\cap\overline{CY}=\{T\}\in$  Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .
<span style="color:#00f">Proof:-</span>   Notice that  $ZI\cdot ZC=ZE\cdot ZD=ZB_1\cdot ZB_2\implies\{Z\}$  has equal powers WRT  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(BIC)$ . So,  $\overline{BZ}$  is the Radical Axis of  $\odot(BIC)$  and  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$ . Similarly we get that  $\overline{CY}$  is the Radical Axis of  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$  and  $\odot(BIC)$ . So taking pairwise radical axis and applying radical axis theorem on  $\odot(BB_1B_2),\odot(CC_1C_2),\odot(BIC)$  we get that  $\overline{BZ}\cap\overline{CY}\in$  Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .

Let  $Ge=\overline{AD}\cap\overline{BE}\cap\overline{CF}$  be the Gregonne Point of  $\Delta ABC$  or the Symmedian Point of  $\Delta DEF$ . Let  $\{L\}\in\overline{YZ}$  such that  $\overline{DL}$  is the  $D-$  Symmedian of  $\Delta DYZ$  ,let  $\overline{YZ}\cap\overline{BC}=\{V\}$ , let  $\overline{BE}\cap\overline{XZ}=\{R\}$  and  $\overline{CF}\cap\overline{XY}=\{S\}$ . Then  $(V,L;Y,Z)=-1\implies\frac{YV}{YL}=\frac{ZV}{ZL}\implies\frac{ZV}{VY}=\frac{ZL}{YL}=\left(\frac{DZ}{DY}\right)^2$ . So, \begin{align*} \frac{ZV}{VY}\cdot\frac{YS}{SX}\cdot\frac{XR}{RZ}=\left(\frac{DZ}{DY}\right)^2\cdot\left(\frac{FY}{FX}\right)^2\cdot\left(\frac{EX}{EZ}\right)^2=1\end{align*}So by Menelaus Theorem on  $\Delta XYZ$  cut by the transversal  $\overline{RS}$  we get that  $\overline{V-S-R}\implies\{\Delta BRZ,\Delta CSY\}$  are perspective. So by Desargues Theorem  $\overline{\overline{BR}\cap\overline{CS},\overline{RZ}\cap\overline{CY},\overline{BZ}\cap\overline{CY}}\implies Ge,X,T$  are collinear. So,  $\{Ge\}$  also lies on the Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .Now notice that  $\overline{XGe}$  is the  $D-$  [SCHWATT LINE](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h621222p3712676) of  $\Delta DEF$ . So,  $\{M\}\in\overline{XGe}$ . So,  $\{M\}$  also lies on the Radical Axis of  $\odot(BB_1B_2)$  and  $\odot(CC_1C_2)$ .   $\blacksquare$ [rule]

Here are two beautiful properties from this problem, the first property is almost direct from the problem and the second one is a bit unrelated but still nice. Here it goes . :)

<blockquote>  $ABC$  be a triangle and let  $\Delta A^*B^*C^*$  be the Tangential Triangle of  $\Delta ABC$  and let  $\Delta DEF$  be the Medial Triangle of  $\Delta ABC$ . Let  $\overline{DE}\cap\odot(ABC)=\{D_1,E_2\}$  ,  $\overline{EF}\cap\odot(ABC)=\{E_1,F_2\}$  and  $\overline{FD}\cap\odot(ABC)=\{F_1,D_2\}$ . Then the Radical Center of  $\odot(A^*F_2E_1),\odot(B^*F_1D_2),\odot(C^*E_2D_1)$  is the Symmedian Point of  $\Delta ABC$ . </blockquote>

<blockquote>  $ABC$  be a triangle with  $\Delta A^*B^*C^*$  as it's tangential triangle and  $\Delta DEF$  as the Medial Triangle of  $\Delta ABC$ . Then the Radical Center of  $\odot(A^*EF),\odot(B^*FD),\odot(C'ED)$  is the Symmedian Point of  $\Delta ABC$ .</blockquote>

