In my humble opinion, the difficulty of this problem is overrated, and particularly that of the complex numbers approach. We present below a "no-stress" complex numbers solution, which in particular avoids the necessity of knowing theorems such as: [https://usamo.wordpress.com/2018/02/28/revisiting-arc-midpoints-in-complex-numbers/](https://usamo.wordpress.com/2018/02/28/revisiting-arc-midpoints-in-complex-numbers/)

Set  $(ABC)$  to the unit circle, and let  $A=1$ . Also let  $B=e^{i\theta}$ ,  $C=e^{-i\theta}$ , and  $P=e^{i\alpha}$ . We set this up so that  $0<\theta<\pi$ ,  $\theta<\alpha<2\pi-\theta$ . This might seem all fancy, but its really just coming from the following picture:
[asy]
