Note that we are defining  $x=e^{i\theta/2}$  and  $y=e^{i\alpha/2}$ . We then see that
\[I_B = \exp \left(i\frac{0+\theta}{2}\right)+\exp \left(i\frac{\theta+\alpha}{2}\right)+\exp \left(i\frac{\alpha+2\pi}{2}\right) = x + xy - y,\]
and
\[I_C = \exp \left(i\frac{\alpha+2\pi-\theta}{2}\right)+\exp \left(i\frac{2\pi-\theta+2\pi}{2}\right)+\exp \left(i\frac{0+\alpha}{2}\right) = -y/x + 1/x + y.\]
What we are doing here is the standard approach, namely that  $I_B$  is the orthocenter of the arc midpoints of  $\widehat{AB},\widehat{BP},\widehat{PA}$ . For  $I_C$  we go in the order  $PC,CA,AB$ .
The difficulty is now gone. We claim the fixed point is  $M=-1$  (the midpoint of arc  $\widehat{BC}$ ). To do that, we have to show that
\[k := \frac{I_B-P}{I_C-P}\div\frac{I_B-M}{I_C-M}\]
is a real number.
We see that
\begin{align*}
k &= \frac{x+xy-y-y^2}{y-y/x+1/x-y^2}\cdot\frac{y-y/x+1/x+1}{x+xy-y+1} 
&= \frac{(1+y)(x-y)}{(1-y)(1+xy)}\cdot\frac{yx-y+1+x}{x+xy-y+1} 
&= \frac{1+y}{1-y}\cdot\frac{x-y}{xy+1},
\end{align*}
so
\[\overline{k}=\frac{1+1/y}{1-1/y}\cdot\frac{1/1x-1/y}{\frac{1}{xy}+1}=\frac{1+y}{y-1}\cdot\frac{y-x}{1+xy} = k.\]
Thus,  $k\in\mathbb{R}$ , so  $I_BI_CPM$  is cyclic, as desired.

