Let  $\triangle ABC$  be inscribed in the unit circle, and furthermore let  $A=1$ . Call the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$   $\omega$ . Define  $J$  and  $K$  as the midpoints of  $\widehat{BP}$  and  $\widehat{CP}$  respectively, and define  $E$  and  $F$  as the second intersections of  $PI_B$  and  $PI_C$  with  $\omega$  respectively. Let a lowercase letter denote the complex number assigned to each point with the respective uppercase letter. Note that  $E$  and  $F$  are the midpoints of  $\widehat{AB}$  and  $\widehat{AC}$  respectively. Thus,  $b=e^2$  and  $c=f^2$ . Furthermore, since  $J$  is the midpoint of  $\widehat{BP}$ ,  $j=\sqrt{bp}=e\sqrt{p}$ . Similarly, taking into account the two roots of  $p$ , we have  $k=-f\sqrt{p}$ . Finally, notice that since  $\triangle ABC$  is isosceles,  $e=\frac{1}{f}$  because they are conjugates. Now, from standard formulas, we have  $$ i_B=\frac{pe(j+1)-j(p+e)}{pe-j}=\frac{pe+\sqrt{p}-p-e}{\sqrt{p}-1} $$   and   $$ i_C=\frac{pf(k+1)-k(p+f)}{pf-k}=\frac{-pf+\sqrt{p}+p+f}{\sqrt{p}+1}. $$   

I claim that  $M$  always lies on the circumcircle of  $\triangle PI_BI_C$ , where  $M$  is the antipode of  $A$ . Since  $m=-1$ , it is sufficient to show that  $\frac{i_B-m}{i_B-p}\cdot\frac{i_C-p}{i_C-m}\in\mathbb{R}$ .  $$ \implies \frac{pe-2\sqrt{p}-p-e-1}{pe-p\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{p}-e}\cdot\frac{-pf+2\sqrt{p}+p+f+1}{-pf-p\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{p}+f}\in\mathbb{R} $$    $$ \implies \frac{pe-2\sqrt{p}-p-e-1}{pe-p\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{p}-e}\cdot\frac{-\frac{p}{e}+2\sqrt{p}+p+\frac{1}{e}+1}{-\frac{p}{e}-p\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{p}+\frac{1}{e}}\in\mathbb{R} $$    $$ \implies \frac{-pe+e\sqrt{p}-\sqrt{p}+1}{-p+e\sqrt{p}-\sqrt{p}+e}\in\mathbb{R}. $$  It is sufficient to show that the LHS is equal to its conjugate. Note that  $$ \overline{\left(\frac{-pe+e\sqrt{p}-\sqrt{p}+1}{-p+e\sqrt{p}-\sqrt{p}+e}\right)}=\frac{-\frac{1}{pe}+\frac{1}{e\sqrt{p}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{p}}+1}{-\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{e\sqrt{p}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{p}}+\frac{1}{e}} $$    $$ =\frac{-1+\sqrt{p}-e\sqrt{p}+pe}{-e+\sqrt{p}-e\sqrt{p}+p}, $$   which is miraculously the same as its conjugate. This finishes the problem.

Let  $M$  be the midpoint of the arc  $AC$  not containing  $A$  and  $M_B,M_C$  be the intersection of  $PI_B,PI_C$  with  $(ABC)$ .**Claim.** We have  $\triangle I_BM_BM\cong \triangle I_CM_CM$  because
\begin{eqnarray*}
MM_B&=&MM_C
\measuredangle MM_BP&=&\measuredangle MM_CP
M_BI_B&=&M_CI_C \hspace{5mm}\text{[fact 5]}
\end{eqnarray*}
So we have   $$ \measuredangle MI_BM_B=\measuredangle MI_CM_C\Longrightarrow \measuredangle MI_BP=\measuredangle MI_CP $$   And we are done. $\square$

