Consider a circle  $\omega$  centered at A with radius AH. Let it intersect the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  at K and L as shown in the figure above.

Claim 1: O lies on KL
Proof:  By cosine rule  $AK^2=OK^2+OA^2-2 \cdot OK\cdot OA \cos(\angle AOK)$    $$ \implies AH^2=2\cdot OA^2 -2\cdot OA^2 \cos(\angle AOK) \iff \angle AOK=\frac{\pi}{2} $$   also we know that since KL is the radical axis of two circles centered at O and A, OA must be perpendicular to KL. Let OA intersect KL at M(not marked in the figure). Then  $\angle AMK=\angle AOK=\frac{\pi}{2}$ . which is only possible if M coincides with O. Hence the claim is true.

Claim 2: P and Q lie on KL
Proof: Power of P with respect to  $\omega= AP^2-AH^2=-PH^2$ . Power of P with respect to (ABC) is  $-PA\cdot PB$  so we only need to prove  $PH^2=PA\cdot PB$ . But this follows since  $\triangle APH \sim \triangle HPB$ . so P lies on the radical axis of  $\omega$  and (ABC) i.e. P lies on KL. Similarly Q lies on KL.
Combining Claim 1 and 2, we are done. 
