Here's a polished version of my solution on the contest, could someone comment? FWIW, this is a very tricky problem to bash, since you can make mistakes and still get the Ceva to work out correctly. This is due to the fact that symmetric a-terms will cancel out. 

Brackets denote area throughout this solution.
I claim that the lines  $I_AO$ ,  $I_BF$ , and  $I_CE$  are concurrent. Let  $I_AO \cap BC = K$ ,  $I_BF \cap AC = H$ ,  $I_CE \cap AB = G$ . Let  $\angle{BAC} = a$  and so on. 

We compute 
\begin{align*}
\frac{AH}{HC} & = \frac{[I_BAF]}{[I_BFC]}   
& = \frac{(I_BA)(AF)\sin{\angle{I_BAF}}}{(I_BC)(CF)\sin{\angle{I_BCF}}}
& = \frac{I_BA}{I_BC} \times \frac{AF}{CF} \times \frac{\sin{\angle{I_BAF}}}{\sin{\angle{I_BCF}}} 
& = \frac{\sin{(90 - c/2)}}{\sin{(90 - a/2)}} \times \cot{A} \times \frac{\sin{(90 + a/2)}}{\sin{(a + c/2)}} 
\end{align*}

Via Symmetry, we may compute that 

\[ \frac{AG}{GB} = \frac{\sin{(90 - b/2)}}{\sin{(90 - a/2)}} \times \cot{A} \times \frac{\sin{(90 + a/2)}}{\sin{(a + b/2)}}  \]

By the Cevian Nest theorem on  $ABC$  wrt  $I_AI_BI_C$ , we'd like to show that 

\[ \frac{AG}{GB} * \frac{BK}{KC} * \frac{CH}{HA} = 1 \] 

which will imply the claim. 

We now find the last ratio,  $\frac{BK}{KC}$ . Observe that 

\begin{align*}
\frac{BK}{KC} & = \frac{[OBI_A]}{[OCI_A]}   
& = \frac{(OB)(BI_A)\sin{\angle{OBI_A}}}{(OC)(CI_A)\sin{\angle{I_ACO}}}
& = \frac{BI_A}{CI_A} \times \frac{\sin{(a + b/2)}}{\sin{{(a + c/2)}}} 
& = \frac{\sin{(90 - c/2)}}{\sin{(90 - b/2)}} \times \frac{\sin{(a + b/2)}}{\sin{(a + c/2)}}
\end{align*}

and at this point \[ \frac{AG}{GB} * \frac{BK}{KC} * \frac{CH}{HA} = 1 \] is clear. Hence, we have shown that  $O$ ,  $I_A$ , and  $P$  are collinear. 

Now all that's left to do is just show that  $YZ \perp OI_A$ . Here is my solution utilizing the (strong) perpendicularity lemma, and I think it is quite nice. Define 
\[ f(X) = P(X, O) - P(X, I_A) \] which is the difference of the powers of  $X$  with respect to the circumcircle and the power with respect to the excircle. 

It is sufficient to show  $f(Y) = f(Z)$ . To do this I'll just show that  $f(Z)$  is symmetric in  $c$  and  $b$  with respect to  $c = AB$ ,  $b = AC$ ,  $a = BC$ . But this is really pretty easy: by straightforward length chasing we have \[ f(Z) = (\frac{a+b+c}{2} - \frac{bc}{a+b})^2 + \frac{(abc^2)}{(a+b)^2} = (\frac{a+b+c}{2})^2 - bc  \] so we're done.

After proving  $OI_A \perp YZ$ , here's a barycentric finish with minimal insight:

We would like to show that  $OI_A, I_BF, I_CE$  are concurrent. To do so, we calculate the equations of these three lines and employ the <details><summary>Concurrence Lemma</summary>The three lines  $u_ix + v_iy + w_iz = 0$ , for  $i = 1, 2, 3$  are concurrent iff \[\begin{vmatrix} u_1 & v_1 & w_1  u_2 & v_2 & w_2  u_3 & v_3 & w_3 \end{vmatrix} = 0.\]</details>. 

Put  $O = \left(a^2S_A : b^2S_B : c^2S_C\right), E = \left(S_{BC} : 0 : S_{AB}\right), F = \left(S_{BC} : S_{CA} : 0\right), I_A = (-a : b : c)$  and similar expressions for  $I_B, I_C.$  Using the determinant formula for a line, we find
\begin{align*}
OI_A &\equiv bc\left(bS_B - cS_C\right)x &&- ac\left(aS_A + cS_C\right)y &&&+ ab\left(aS_A + bS_B\right)z &&&&= 0  
I_BF &\equiv  -cS_{CA}x &&+ cS_{BC}y &&&+ S_C\left(aS_A + bS_B\right)z &&&&= 0  
I_CE &\equiv bS_{AB}x &&- S_B\left(aS_A + cS_C\right)y &&&- bS_{BC}z &&&&= 0.
\end{align*}
By the Concurrence Lemma, it remains to show that 
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} bc\left(bS_B - cS_C\right) & -ac\left(aS_A + cS_C\right) & ab\left(aS_A + bS_B\right)  
-cS_{CA} & cS_{BC} & S_C\left(aS_A + bS_B\right)   
bS_{AB} & - S_B\left(aS_A + cS_C\right) & -bS_{BC} \end{vmatrix} = 0.
\end{align*}
Factoring  $S_C$  and  $S_B$  out of the second and third rows respectively, it suffices to show that 
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} bc\left(bS_B - cS_C\right) & -ac\left(aS_A + cS_C\right) & ab\left(aS_A + bS_B\right)  
-cS_A & cS_B & aS_A + bS_B   
bS_A & - aS_A - cS_C & -bS_C \end{vmatrix} = 0.
\end{align*}
Subtracting  $ab$  times the second row from the first, the above expression equals 
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} bc\left(aS_A + bS_B - cS_C\right) & -ac\left(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C\right) & 0  
-cS_A & cS_B & aS_A + bS_B   
bS_A & - aS_A - cS_C & -bS_C \end{vmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Now expand by the first row. Evaluating minors, the above expression equals
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} bc\left(aS_A + bS_B - cS_C\right) & -ac\left(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C\right)  
bS_A\left(-aS_A - bS_B + cS_C\right) & aS_A\left(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C\right) \end{vmatrix},
\end{align*}
which is clearly  $0$ , as desired.

