Let  $B',C'$  denote the reflections of  $I_A$  in  $AB,AC$ . Let  $I_A$  be the  $A$ -excenter, and  $J$  the circumcenter of  $\triangle II_BI_C$ . Note first that  $O,J,I_A$  lie on the Euler line of  $\triangle II_BI_C$ . **Lemma.**  $OJ\perp YZ$ . 

*Proof.* We show that  $YZ$  is the radical axis of  $(ABC),(II_BI_C)$ , from which the lemma follows. Considering the power of  $Y$  in  $(ICI_BA)$ , we have  $YC\cdot YA=YI\cdot YI_B$ , and similarly  $ZA\cdot ZB=ZI\cdot ZI_C$ , so the result follows.  $\square$ It suffices to show that  $I_A,P,J$  are collinear. First, since  $C(A,B;I_A,I_C)=-1$  and  $BE\perp AC$ , we have that  $CA$  (externally) bisects  $\angle I_AEI_C$ , and similarly  $AB$  (externally) bisects  $\angle I_AFI_B$ .

Thus  $B',C'$  lie on  $I_BF,I_CE$  respectively. Note that  $\triangle I_AB'C'$  and  $\triangle JI_BI_C$  are both isosceles, and   $$ \angle B'I_AC'=180^{\circ}-\angle BAC=2\angle I_BI_AI_C=\angle I_BJI_C, $$   so  $\triangle I_AB'C'\sim\triangle JI_BI_C$ , and since  $B'C'\perp AI_A\perp I_BI_C$ , these two triangles are homothetic at  $P$ . Thus  $P$  lies on  $\overline{JI_A}$ , as required.

