The following solution has two key claims.

<u>Claim 1:</u>  $I_A,O,P$  are collinear.
<u>Claim 2:</u>  $I_AO\perp YZ$ .

Clearly if we take these two toegether, then the problem is solved. We now prove each claim with a computation.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<u>Proof of Claim 1:</u> To prove this claim, we will want to view the configuration in terms of the excentral triangle  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$ . In fact, the angles  $A,B,C$  represent the angles of that triangle (not  $\triangle ABC$ ), and similarly  $a,b,c$  represent its side lengths.

To prove the claim, we will want to use trig Ceva. To do this, we must compute
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}.\]
By the law of sines on  $\triangle BEI_C$ , we have that
\[\frac{EI_C}{\sin\angle EBI_C}=\frac{EB}{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}.\]
Also, by the law of sines on  $\triangle AEI_C$ , we have that
\[\frac{EI_C}{\sin\angle EAI_C}=\frac{EA}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}.\]
Dividing, we see that
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}=\frac{EB}{EA}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle EBI_C}{\sin\angle EAI_C}.\]
But note that  $\angle EAI_C=\pi-A$ , and
\[\angle EBI_C = \angle EBA+\angle ABI_C = (\pi/2-\angle EAB)+B = (\pi/2-(\pi-2A))+B=\pi/2+A-C,\]
so
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}=\frac{EB}{EA}\cdot\frac{\cos(A-C)}{\sin A}.\]
Similarly, we derive
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_BP}{\sin\angle I_CI_BP}=\frac{FC}{FA}\cdot\frac{\cos(A-B)}{\sin A}.\]
Finally,  $O$  is the nine point center of  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$ , so with respect to that triangle, it has barycentric coordinates  $O=(a\cos(B-C):b\cos(C-A):c\cos(A-B))$ , so by the ratio lemma,
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_BI_AO}{\sin\angle I_CI_AO}=\frac{\cos(A-C)}{\cos(A-B)}.\]
Therefore, we have that
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle I_CI_BP}{\sin\angle I_AI_BP}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle I_BI_AO}{\sin\angle I_CI_AO}=\frac{EB}{EA}\div\frac{FC}{FA}.\]
But note that  $EB/EA=FC/FA=\tan\angle CAB$ , so
\[\frac{\sin\angle I_AI_CP}{\sin\angle I_BI_CP}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle I_CI_BP}{\sin\angle I_AI_BP}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle I_BI_AO}{\sin\angle I_CI_AO}=1,\]
so by trig Ceva, we have that  $I_AO,I_BF,I_CE$  concur. Therefore,  $P,O,I_A$  collinear.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<u>Proof of Claim 2:</u> We now shift the focus to  $\triangle ABC$ . In particular, we employ barycentric coordinates in  $\triangle ABC$ , so the angles  $A,B,C$  and sides  $a,b,c$  now retain their usual meaning (different than in the proof of claim 1). Note that
\[Y=\left(\frac{a}{a+c},0,\frac{c}{a+c}\right)\text{ and }Z=\left(\frac{a}{a+b},\frac{b}{a+b},0\right).\]
Thus, the displacement vector
\[\vec{ZY}=\frac{1}{(a+c)(a+b)}(a(b-c),-b(a+c),c(a+b)),\]
and we also have
\[\vec{OI_A} = \frac{1}{b+c-a}(b\vec{OB}+c\vec{OC}-a\vec{OA}).\]
Therefore, by strong EFFT, it suffices to verify that
\[a^2(-bc(a+c)+bc(a+b))+b^2(ac(b-c)-ac(a+b))+c^2(ab(b-c)+ab(a+c))=0,\]
which is evident upon expansion. This completes the proof of Claim 2.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Combining these two claims shows that  $PO\perp YZ$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

