<details><summary>proof of the lemma</summary>here we use complex numbers:
Let:
\[a=x^2,b=y^2,c=z^2,q=-xz,r=-xy\]

\[e=\frac{ac(b+q)-bq(a+c)}{ac-bq}=\frac{x^2\:y^2\:-\:x^2\:z^2\:+\:x\:y^2\:z\:+\:y^2\:z^2}{x\:z\:+\:y^2}\]
and similarly we have:
\[f=\frac{-x^2\:y^2\:+\:x^2\:z^2\:+\:x\:y\:z^2\:+\:y^2\:z^2}{x\:y\:+\:z^2}\]

now we should prove that  $\frac{e-f}{i_a-o} \in i\mathbb R$ :

\[\frac{e-f}{i_a-o} =-\frac{\left(x\:+\:y\right)\:\left(y\:-\:z\right)\:\left(x\:+\:z\right)\:\left(y\:+\:z\right)}{\left(y^2\:+\:x\:z\right)\:\left(x\:y\:+\:z^2\right)}=-\frac{\left(\frac{1}{x}\:+\:\frac{1}{y}\right)\:\left(\frac{1}{y}\:-\:\frac{1}{z}\right)\:\left(\frac{1}{x}\:+\:\frac{1}{z}\right)\:\left(\frac{1}{y}\:+\:\frac{1}{z}\right)}{\left(\frac{1}{y^2}\:+\:\frac{1}{xz}\right)\:\left(\frac{1}{xz}\:+\:\frac{1}{z^2}\right)}=-\overline{(\frac{e-f}{i_a-o})}\]

hence we are done.</details>

now if we combine the  $\color{green} \textbf{claim 1}$  and  $\color{green} \textbf{Lemma}$  we can easily conclude that we have proven the problem. $\blacksquare$

