Similarly, Legendre's Formula is a well-known formula that we can cite. However, the same cannot be said for Hook Length... I kinda feel like you are comparing apples to oranges here (or maybe little apples to really big apples)...?

@Above Thank you, I cleared my search history after being so... enlightened. 

Anyway, we just need to observe that -   $$  \lfloor a \rfloor - \lfloor b \rfloor \geq \lfloor a - b \rfloor \geq - \lfloor b - a \rfloor   $$  so, indeed -   $$  \lfloor \frac{j}{p^i} \rfloor - \lfloor \frac{j + k}{p^i} \rfloor \geq - \lfloor \frac{k}{p^i} \rfloor   $$  multiplying this by  $k - 1$  gives us the lower bound, and it can be verified that -   $$  \lfloor \frac{k^2}{p^i} \rfloor - \lfloor \frac{k(k - 1)}{p^i} \rfloor \geq  \lfloor \frac{k}{p^i} \rfloor   $$  summing over all  $i$  gives us the result. 

Edit: Huh, weird, the post is gone/edited. I could elaborate but I am afraid that I too, would fall victim to the ever efficient judgement of this forum's moderators.

@trumpeter: I don't think it's actually ridiculously counterintuitive. We have to have a guarantee, and are facing an enemy who has more choice the higher  $k$  is. The only compensation you get for that is that we have more cards we can check at a time.

Add in the incremental solution (determine the first card, then the next, and then the next, etc.) and it's clear that the former consideration (higher choice for the enemy) is more important than having more cards we can check at a time.

The probabilistic things is probably why they specified  $m$ , just to get rid of the people who claim that winning in the limit is good enough (of course, it isn't). Or perhaps there's an incomputable strategy that exists but we cannot know which works after a length of time that we cannot compute when  $k = n$ .

