Let  $OI \cap BC \equiv D$ . Also, let  $E$  and  $F$  be the foots of  $I$  and  $O$  on  $BC$  respectively.
WLOG, let  $\overline{AM}=\overline{MN}=\overline{NP}=\overline{PQ}=\overline{QA}=1$ .
Also, let  $\angle BNM=\alpha$  and  $\angle CPQ=\beta$ .
Then, applying cosine rule in  $\triangle BNM$  and  $\triangle CPQ$  yields  $BN=(c-1) \cos B+\cos \alpha$  and  $CP=(b-1) \cos C+\cos \beta$ .
Also, we have  $a=1+BN+CP \implies$    $$ \cos \alpha+\cos \beta=\cos B+\cos C-1. $$  Also, by sine rule in  $\triangle BMN$ ,  $\frac{c-1}{\sin \alpha}=\frac{1}{\sin B}$ . Similarly, in  $\triangle CPQ$ ,  $\frac{b-1}{\sin \beta}=\frac{1}{\sin C}$ . Then, we get   $$ \sin \beta-\sin \alpha=\sin B-\sin C. $$  Now, \begin{align*}
\tan \left (\frac{\beta -\alpha}{2} \right)&=\frac{\sin \left (\frac{\beta-\alpha}{2} \right)}{\cos \left (\frac{\beta-\alpha}{2} \right)}
=\frac{\sin B-\sin C}{2 \cos \left (\frac{\beta+\alpha}{2} \right) \cdot \cos \left (\frac{\beta-\alpha}{2} \right)}
&=\frac{\sin B-\sin C}{\cos \alpha+\cos \beta}=\frac{\sin B-\sin C}{\cos B+\cos C-1}.
\end{align*}
Now, we know that  $\angle (\ell,BC)=90^\circ+\frac{\beta-\alpha}{2}$ . Also, if  $\angle (OI,BC)=\angle IDB=\gamma$  and  $EF=x$ , then   $$ \frac{OF}{IE}=\frac{FD}{ED}
\implies \frac{R \cos A}{r}=\frac{x}{EF+x}
\implies \tan \gamma=\frac{r}{DE}=\frac{r-R \cos A}{\frac{1}{2}(b-c)}. $$  Now, to prove  $OI || \ell$ , we only need to show that   $$ \frac{r-R \cos A}{\frac{1}{2}(b-c)}=\frac{\cos B+\cos C-1}{\sin B-\sin C}. $$  Then, substituting  $b=2R \sin B$  and  $c=2R \sin C$  and simplifying, we get   $$ \frac{r}{R}=\cos A+\cos B+\cos C-1 $$   which we already know that it is true.
Hence,  $\overline{OI} || \ell$ . $\blacksquare$</details>
