<details><summary>My solution</summary>First, we prove that 
\[\sqrt{k^2 + l^2 - 2kl \cos \theta} \ge (k+l) \sqrt{\frac{1 - \cos\theta}{2}}.\]
whenever  $k$  and  $l$  are positive and  $0^\circ \le \theta \le 180^\circ$  and that equality occurs whenever  $k = l$ . To prove this, note that 
\begin{align*}
(k - l)^2(1 + \cos\theta) &\ge 0 
(k^2 - 2kl + l^2) + (k^2 - 2kl + l^2) \cos \theta  &\ge 0 
2k^2 + 2l^2 -  (k + l)^2 + ((k+l)^2 - 4kl) \cos\theta  &\ge 0 
2k^2 + 2l^2 - 4kl \cos \theta  &\ge (k+l)^2(1 - \cos \theta) 
k^2 + l^2 - 2kl \cos \theta & \ge \frac{1}{2} (k + l)^2(1 - \cos\theta) 
\sqrt{k^2 + l^2 - 2kl \cos \theta}  &\ge (k+l) \sqrt{\frac{1 - \cos\theta}{2}}.
\end{align*}
In addition, when  $k = l$ , the left side becomes 
\[\sqrt{k^2 + l^2 - 2kl \cos \theta} = \sqrt{2k^2 - 2k^2 \cos \theta} = k\sqrt{2}(\sqrt{1 - \cos\theta})\] and the right side becomes 
\[(k + l) \sqrt{\frac{1- \cos\theta}{2}} = 2k \sqrt{\frac{1- \cos\theta}{2}} = k\sqrt{2}(\sqrt{1- \cos\theta})\]
so equality holds. 

