Note that the closer angle  $\theta$  is to  $90^\circ$ , the heights of triangle  $ABC$  and  $ADC$  become larger and the area of the quadrilateral is maximized. It it tempting to assume then that  $\theta = 90^\circ$ , but we also see that the perimeter of  $5/2$  must be achieveable, which is not in the case of  $\theta = 90^\circ$ . Thus, our next step is to compute the angles  $\theta$  for which the perimeter of  $5/2$  is achieveable. This is where our lemma comes in. Note that the length of each sides are
\begin{align*}
AB & = \sqrt{k^2 + l^2 - 2kl \cos\theta}
BC & = \sqrt{(1 - k)^2 + l^2 - 2(1 - k)l \cos\theta}
CD & = \sqrt{(1 - k)^2 + (1 - l)^2 - 2(1 -k)l \cos \theta}
DA & = \sqrt{k^2 + (1 - l)^2 - 2k(1-l)\cos\theta}
\end{align*}
By the lemma we proved in the beginning, we have
\begin{align}
AB & \ge (k + l)\sqrt{\frac{1 - \cos \theta}{2}}
BC & \ge (1 - k + l)\sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos \theta}{2}}
CD & \ge (2 - k - l)\sqrt{\frac{1 - \cos \theta}{2}}
DA & \ge (k + 1 - l)\sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos\theta}{2}}
\end{align}
Sum (1) and (3) to get
\[AB + CD \ge 2\sqrt{\frac{1 - \cos\theta}{2}}\]
and (2) and (4) to get
\[BC + DA \ge 2\sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos\theta}{2}}\]
so
\begin{align*}
P & = AB + BC + CD + DA \ge \sqrt{2(1 - \cos\theta)} + \sqrt{2( 1 + \cos\theta)} 
P^2 & \ge 2(1 - \cos\theta) + 2\sqrt{4(1 - \cos\theta)(1 + \cos\theta)} + 2(1 + \cos\theta) 
& = 4 + 4 \sqrt{ 1 - \cos\theta }
& = 4 + 4 \sin \theta 
\end{align*}
Without loss of generality, assume that  $\theta$  is acute. Then as  $\theta$  increases,  $\sin \theta$  and  $P^2$  increases, so the minimal value of  $P$  increases. When  $P = 5/2$ , 
\[\left(\frac{5}{2}\right)^2 = 4 + 4\sin \theta \implies \sin \theta = 9/16,\]
and the acute angle  $\theta$  for which the above is true is produces the quadrilateral with the maximal possible angle, since we pointed out in the beginning that by increasing the angle, we can increase the area. In addition, this minimal value is possible when  $k = l = \frac{1}{2}$ . 

Now, we find the area of such a quadrilateral. Note that the length of the altitude to  $AC$  of triangle  $ABC$  in figure 2 is  $l\sin \theta$  and that of  $ADC$  is  $(1- l)\sin \theta$ . The base of each triangle is  $1$ . Thus, the area is
\begin{align*}
[ABC] + [ADC] & = \frac{1}{2}l\sin\theta + \frac{1}{2}(1- l)\sin\theta 
& = \frac{1}{2}(\sin\theta)(1) 
& = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{9}{16}\right) 
& = \boxed{ \frac{9}{32} }
\end{align*}</details>

