We first consider the recurrence  $y_{k+3} - 3y_{k+2} + 3y_{k+1} - y_{k} = 0$ . We now manipulate its characteristic polynomial, given by  $y^3-3y^2+3y-1=0$ .
\begin{align*}
    0 &= y^3 - 3y^2 + 3y - 1 
    &= (y-1)^3 
    y &= 1
\end{align*}
Since this characteristic polynomial has only 1 distinct root, we can reasonably conclude that   $$ y_k = ak^2+bk+c $$   for suitable constants  $a,b,c$ . We can now solve for  $a,b,c$  by using the other conditions given in the problem. We start first with the condition that   $$ \sum_{k=1}^n y_k = 0 $$   Substituting gives us 
\begin{align*}
    0 &= a\sum_{k=1}^n k^2 + b\sum_{k=1}^n k + cn 
    &= \frac{an(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} + \frac{bn(n+1)}{2} + cn 
    &= a(2n^3+3n^2+n) + 3b(n^2+n) +6cn
\end{align*}
Now we proceed by equating coefficients, which yields the equations 
\begin{align*}
    a+3b+6c &= 0 
    3a+3b &= 0 
    2a &= 0
\end{align*}
Hence from here, we can conclude that  $a = b = c = 0$ , from which it follows that  $y_k = 0$  for all  $k$ . By this, it is trivially true that  $\sum_{k=1}^n k^3y_k = 0$ .  $\blacksquare$ For me this felt like the easiest problem on the test. My solution used a quadratic in  $k$ .

